{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak to Aaron your brother.</b> Why did He see fit to mention brotherhood here? Rather, to tell you that Aaron should not rely upon the brotherhood, saying to himself that he would not die when he enters the Holy on the merit of Moses his brother, lest he be consumed as half his flesh — as Rashi explained in parashat בְּהַעֲלֹתְךָ (Numbers 12:12) on the verse ״וַיֵּאָכֵל חֲצִי בְּשָׂרוֹ״. Therefore He said: even though he is your brother, nevertheless do not rely on this at all. Or He said: since he is your brother, and if he dies it is as though half your own flesh were consumed, therefore the obligation to warn him falls upon you all the more. And some say: even though he is your brother in prophecy, and it is written at the giving of the Torah ״וְעָלִיתָ אַתָּה וְאַהֲרֹן״ (Exodus 19:24), he might come to think: just as Moses is permitted to enter at all times, for ״בְּכָל בֵּיתִי נֶאֱמָן הוּא״ (Numbers 12:7) — like a trusted household attendant who enters his master's house whenever he wishes — so too may he himself enter; therefore you must warn him all the more." "<b>And he shall not come at all times into the Holy.</b> The expression ״בְּכָל עֵת״ is imprecise, for it implies that at all times he shall not enter, but at intervals he may — and the truth is not so, for he enters only once a year. And I say that the reason for the prohibition of his entry there depends on the sin of the people, for their transgressions separate between them and their Elohim, and their corruption prevents even the entry of the priest, for the guilt of the people depends upon him, as it is written (Exodus 28:1): ״וְאַתָּה הַקְרֵב אֵלֶיךָ אֶת אַהֲרֹן אָחִיךָ מִתּוֹךְ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — this teaches that he earned this bringing near only from among the children of Israel, and when Israel are not worthy, the priest who depends upon them is not worthy to see the face of the Shekhinah either, except on Yom HaKippurim, when all Israel are likened on that day to the ministering angels, who are above time and over whom time has no dominion. Therefore this holy day is as though it does not fall within the category of time and season, for all the days of the year are within the category of עֵת, that is, time, except for this day." "And a hint to the matter: the שָׂטָן rises to three hundred sixty-four, and the days of the sun number three hundred sixty-five, to teach that on all the days of the year, when the שָׂטָן and the evil inclination hold sway over all creatures, they are included within the category of עֵת — except for this day, on which the שָׂטָן has no dominion; therefore it is not included in the עֵת of all the days of the year but stands on its own, set apart from all the days of the year. And when He said ״וְאַל יָבֹא בְּכָל עֵת״, He hinted that he shall not enter at all on any day that is included within the category of עֵת, but only on a day that is not included within the category of עֵת — and that is the day of pardon and forgiveness, on which Israel are likened to the ministering angels who do not fall under the dominion of עֵת, and which is not included among the days of the year over which the שָׂטָן, who causes sin, holds sway; and therefore it is fitting that their emissary should come to see the face of the Shekhinah. And He gave the reason for the matter: ״כִּי בֶּעָנָן אֵרָאֶה״ — that is to say, had there been an explicit revelation of My Shekhinah, I would not have needed to prevent him from entering there, for ״לֹא יִרְאַנִי הָאָדָם וָחָי״ (Exodus 33:20); but since My Shekhinah is in the cloud, he is able to see My glory, for the cloud is the cause for this seeing. Therefore it says ״כִּי בֶּעָנָן אֵרָאֶה״, and therefore I must prevent him from the seeing on all the days of the year, when Israel are habituated in transgressions."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:51.982094083Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}